# The 4% Rule Calculator (4percentcalc.com)

> Free, open-source retirement withdrawal backtester. Tests a starting portfolio and withdrawal rate against every historical start year from <%= @first_year %> to <%= @last_year %> using Robert Shiller's S&P 500, 10-year Treasury and CPI data, plus Nasdaq Composite from 1972.

## How the calculation works

- Withdraw at the start of each year.
- The first withdrawal is portfolio × rate; each following year's withdrawal is the previous one × (1 + that year's inflation).
- After the withdrawal, apply the year's portfolio return, then subtract the annual fee from the year-end balance.
- Mixed portfolios (stocks/10-year Treasuries) rebalance to the target mix every year.
- A start year "fails" if the balance hits zero before the horizon. Success rate = share of start years with at least horizon years of data that did not fail.

## API (JSON, no auth, 60 req/min/IP)

Identical calls are cached for 24 hours.

- [GET /api/v1/backtest](<%= request.base_url %>/api/v1/backtest?initial_amount=1000000&withdrawal_rate=4.0&fee=0&scenario=sp500&horizon=30): success rate summary plus a result for every start year.
- [GET /api/v1/backtest/:starting_year](<%= request.base_url %>/api/v1/backtest/1966?initial_amount=1000000&withdrawal_rate=4.0): one start year, with a year-by-year `series` of portfolio values (year 0 is the starting balance).
- [GET /api/v1/data](<%= request.base_url %>/api/v1/data): the raw yearly data (S&P 500, 10-year Treasury, inflation, Nasdaq returns) and the scenario list.

Backtest parameters (all optional):

- `initial_amount`: starting portfolio in dollars, 1 to 1,000,000,000 (default 1000000).
- `annual_spending`: first-year spending in dollars; if present, the starting portfolio is derived as spending / rate.
- `withdrawal_rate`: percent, 0.5 to 20 (default 4.0).
- `fee`: annual fee in percent, 0 to 3 (default 0).
- `scenario`: sp500 (default), sp75, sp50, sp25, bonds, nasdaq (starts in 1972).
- `horizon`: years, one of 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 (default 30).

Out-of-range values return HTTP 400 with `{"error": "..."}`. Unknown start years return 404.

